<Project>

  <!--
    `spikes/` is outside `Okf.sln` on purpose (AD-43): nothing under it ships, no
    CI job builds it, and a throwaway that already answered its question is not
    worth an analyzer sweep. The repo-root `Directory.Build.props` reaches it
    anyway — it is above it in the tree — so without this a spike build would
    fail on findings nobody is going to act on (CA2100 on the spike's own SQL,
    CA1031 on its `Main`).

    Two things stay exactly as the root file set them, deliberately: `Nullable`,
    and `TreatWarningsAsErrors` for the COMPILER's own diagnostics. A spike that
    stops compiling cleanly is still a broken spike.

    This is MSBuild rather than a `[spikes/**/*.cs]` section in the root
    `.editorconfig` because a path-scoped section cannot express it. The bulk key
    `dotnet_analyzer_diagnostic.severity` is the only way to name every rule at
    once, and a bulk key loses to a specific one — `AnalysisLevel=latest-all` is
    implemented as a per-rule global AnalyzerConfig the SDK generates, so its
    `dotnet_diagnostic.CA2100.severity = warning` outranks any bulk line written
    here. Verified by building the spike with such a section in place and
    watching CA2100 come through it regardless. The per-rule lines in the root
    `.editorconfig` have no such problem, which is why `[tests/**/*.cs]` works.
  -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
